**چرا این کد کار می‌کند:**
- `dir="rtl"` روی تگ `<html>` کل چیدمان را راست‌به‌چپ می‌کند و ویژگی‌های منطقی مثل `margin-inline-start` جهت را خودکار جابه‌جا می‌کنند.
- فونت «Vazirmatn» با دو `preconnect` زودتر از `stylesheet` لود می‌شود تا تأخیر فونت کم شود و `display=swap` از متن نامرئی جلوگیری می‌کند.
- `clamp()` اندازهٔ تیتر را بین دو حد با نرخ واکنش‌گرا نگه می‌دارد، بدون media query.
- منوی موبایل فقط با `classList.toggle` باز و بسته می‌شود و `aria-expanded` برای صفحه‌خوان‌ها به‌روز می‌ماند.

با margin بین آیتم‌ها حلش می‌کنیم — بدون نیاز به gap:

```css
.toolbar {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
}

.toolbar > * + * {
  margin-inline-start: 12px;
}
```

ترفند `> * + *` یعنی «هر فرزند به‌جز اولی» فاصله بگیره. مزیتش اینه که:

- نیازی به کلاس اضافه روی دکمه‌ها نیست
- اگه تعداد دکمه‌ها عوض بشه هم فاصله‌ها درست می‌مونن (برخلاف روش margin-left + حذف margin از آخرین آیتم)
- چون از `margin-inline-start` استفاده کردیم، توی چیدمان RTL هم جهت فاصله خودکار درست می‌شه

اگه نسخهٔ مرورگرت واقعاً قدیمیه، `margin-inline-start` رو با `margin-left: 12px` جایگزین کن (فقط یادت باشه تو RTL باید منطق برعکس بشه).

کد کامل scroll spy:

```js
const links = document.querySelectorAll('.toc a');
const map = new Map();

for (const link of links) {
  const section = document.querySelector(link.hash);
  if (section) map.set(section, link);
}

const observer = new IntersectionObserver(
  (entries) => {
    for (const entry of entries) {
      if (entry.isIntersecting) {
        links.forEach((l) => l.classList.remove('active'));
        map.get(entry.target).classList.add('active');
      }
    }
  },
  { rootMargin: '-40% 0px -55% 0px' }
);

map.forEach((_, section) => observer.observe(section));
```

```css
.toc a.active { color: #2563eb; font-weight: 700; }
```

**چطور کار می‌کنه:**
- `rootMargin: '-40% 0px -55% 0px'` یه نوار باریک حدوداً وسط viewport می‌سازه؛ هر section که وارد این نوار بشه «فعال» محسوب می‌شه — به‌جای کل صفحه، حس دقیق‌تری می‌ده
- IntersectionObserver خیلی سبک‌تر از scroll event هست چون بعد از رندر اطلاع می‌ده، نه اینکه هر فریم محاسبه کنه
- `link.hash` مقدار `#about` رو می‌ده و `document.querySelector` همون section رو پیدا می‌کنه

```css
.card {
  transition: transform 0.3s ease;
}

.card:hover {
  transform: translateY(-6px) scale(1.02);
}

.badge {
  /* center without knowing the element's size */
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%;
  left: 50%;
  translate: -50% -50%;
}
```

**Why this works:** `transform` applies an affine transformation (translate, rotate, scale, skew) to the element's rendered box *after* layout is computed — the element still occupies its original space, so transforms never trigger reflow of surrounding content. Because the transformed result is a compositor-layer operation (when transitioned on its own), the browser can hand it to the GPU and animate at 60fps even during heavy page paint. Moving with `top`/`left` instead recalculates layout every frame, and scaling with `width`/`height` reflows the whole document — both are the classic causes of janky hover effects. Transforms compose right-to-left in the listed order, so `translateY(-6px) scale(1.02)` first scales, then shifts. The independent `translate` property (note: no `transform()` wrapper) combined with `top/left: 50%` is the size-agnostic centering trick: `50%` of the parent minus `50%` of the element's own box.

**Symptom:** `SyntaxError: Unexpected token '<', "<html>" is not valid JSON` (or `Unexpected token o`, `Unexpected end of JSON input`).

**Root cause:** you are parsing something that is not JSON. `<` at position 0 means the server returned an HTML page (login redirect, 404/500 error page, SPA index.html fallback) — usually a wrong API path, an expired session, or a proxy answering instead of the backend. `Unexpected token o` usually means the value was already an object (stringified as `[object Object]`). `Unexpected end of input` means a truncated or empty body.

**Diagnosis:**
```js
const res = await fetch('/api/data');
console.log(res.status, res.headers.get('content-type'));
const raw = await res.text();      // look at the ACTUAL body first
console.log(raw.slice(0, 200));
const data = JSON.parse(raw);      // now parse
```

**Fix — parse defensively:**
```js
async function getJSON(url) {
  const res = await fetch(url);
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}: ${await res.text().catch(() => '')}`);
  const type = res.headers.get('content-type') ?? '';
  if (!type.includes('application/json')) {
    throw new Error(`Expected JSON, got ${type || 'unknown'}`);
  }
  return res.json();
}
```

**Prevention:** never call `res.json()` before checking `res.ok`; verify the route, method and auth headers; check `Content-Type` before parsing; handle `res.status === 204` explicitly since it has no body.

```css
.alert {
  /* Physical (direction-blind): */
  margin-left: auto;
  padding-left: 1rem;
  border-left: 4px solid currentColor;
  text-align: left;

  /* Logical (adapts to writing mode): */
  margin-inline-start: auto;
  padding-inline-start: 1rem;
  border-inline-start: 4px solid currentColor;
  text-align: start;
}

/* Axis shorthand pair: */
.section {
  padding-block: 2rem;   /* top + bottom */
  margin-inline: auto;   /* left + right */
}
```

**Why this works:** Physical properties (`left`, `right`, `top`, `bottom`) are pinned to the screen's geometry, but in right-to-left languages like Persian and Arabic "left" and "right" swap meaning — a card built with `padding-left` looks correct in English and broken in RTL. Logical properties map to *flow-relative* axes: `inline` is the axis text flows along, `block` is the axis lines stack along, and `start`/`end` flip automatically with `direction` or `writing-mode`. The exact same stylesheet therefore mirrors itself for free under `dir="rtl"`, with no second RTL stylesheet and no `[dir="rtl"]` override blocks. `inset-inline-start` likewise replaces `left` for positioned elements. The `*-block`/`*-inline` shorthands also set both sides in one declaration. New projects should standardize on logical properties; the physical ones remain only for genuinely screen-anchored cases like `position: fixed` overlays relative to the viewport.

```html
<div class='tabs'>
  <div role='tablist' aria-label='Sections'>
    <button role='tab' id='t1' aria-controls='p1' aria-selected='true'>One</button>
    <button role='tab' id='t2' aria-controls='p2' aria-selected='false'>Two</button>
    <button role='tab' id='t3' aria-controls='p3' aria-selected='false'>Three</button>
  </div>
  <div role='tabpanel' id='p1' aria-labelledby='t1'>Panel one</div>
  <div role='tabpanel' id='p2' aria-labelledby='t2' hidden>Panel two</div>
  <div role='tabpanel' id='p3' aria-labelledby='t3' hidden>Panel three</div>
</div>
```

```css
[role='tab'] { padding: 0.5rem 1rem; border: 0; background: none; cursor: pointer; font: inherit; }
[role='tab'][aria-selected='true'] { border-bottom: 2px solid #4f46e5; font-weight: 600; }
[role='tabpanel'] { padding: 1rem; border: 1px solid #e5e7eb; border-top: 0; }
```

```js
const tabs = [...document.querySelectorAll('[role=tab]')];
const panels = [...document.querySelectorAll('[role=tabpanel]')];

function activate(tab) {
  tabs.forEach(t => {
    const on = t === tab;
    t.setAttribute('aria-selected', on);
    document.getElementById(t.getAttribute('aria-controls')).hidden = !on;
  });
  tab.focus();
}

tabs.forEach(tab => {
  tab.addEventListener('click', () => activate(tab));
  tab.addEventListener('keydown', e => {
    const i = tabs.indexOf(tab);
    if (e.key === 'ArrowRight') activate(tabs[(i + 1) % tabs.length]);
    if (e.key === 'ArrowLeft') activate(tabs[(i - 1 + tabs.length) % tabs.length]);
  });
});
```

Why it works: the ARIA wiring (tablist/tab/tabpanel, aria-selected, aria-controls) is what screen readers rely on to announce 'Tab 2 of 3, selected'. Hidden panels are removed with the hidden attribute rather than CSS, so they cannot leak through a stylesheet change. Arrow-key navigation with wrap-around follows the WAI-ARIA tabs pattern, and activate() is a single source of truth that both clicks and keyboard events call, so the two input paths can never disagree.

```css
.card {
  background: #fff;
  border-radius: 12px;
  box-shadow:
    0 1px 2px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.06),
    0 4px 12px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.08),
    0 12px 32px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.10);
}
```

Why it works: real shadows are not one shadow — they are an ambient shadow (light from all directions, tight and faint) plus a key shadow (from above, larger and softer). Stacking three box-shadows from tight to wide with very low alpha values reproduces that falloff, which the eye reads as soft and physical. The common mistake is a single shadow like 0 4px 10px rgba(0,0,0,.5): high opacity with small blur equals harsh. Two rules of thumb: large blur + low alpha, and never pure black — a slightly tinted color like rgba(15,23,42,.12) looks even more natural.

repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr)) creates as many ≥240px columns as fit, letting each grow equally. auto-fit collapses empty tracks, so with one card it stretches full width; auto-fill keeps empty tracks reserved.

```css
.grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr));
  gap: 1rem;
}
```

