```js
document.querySelectorAll('.item').forEach(item => {
  const head = item.querySelector('.head');
  const body = item.querySelector('.body');
  head.addEventListener('click', () => {
    const open = head.getAttribute('aria-expanded') === 'true';
    head.setAttribute('aria-expanded', String(!open));
    if (open) {
      body.style.height = body.scrollHeight + 'px';
      requestAnimationFrame(() => { body.style.height = '0px'; });
    } else {
      body.style.height = body.scrollHeight + 'px';
      body.addEventListener('transitionend', function done(e) {
        if (e.propertyName === 'height') {
          body.style.height = 'auto';
          body.removeEventListener('transitionend', done);
        }
      });
    }
  });
});
```

Why it works: CSS cannot transition to height:auto, so the pattern is: measure scrollHeight, set it as an explicit pixel height (which is transitionable), then on open flip it to auto after the transition ends so the panel resizes with content later. Closing does the reverse — pin to the measured height first, then drop to 0 inside requestAnimationFrame so the browser registers the start value before the transition begins. aria-expanded keeps assistive tech informed, and overflow:hidden clips the content during the animation.

```css
.card-grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns:
    repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(260px, 100%), 1fr));
  gap: 1.5rem;
}
```

**Why this works:** This is the auto-responsive pattern: `minmax(260px, 1fr)` says every column must be *at least* 260px but may grow to share the leftover space equally. `auto-fit` asks the browser to fit as many such columns as possible into the container and collapse any empty ones. On a 1000px container you get 3 columns (1000/260 → 3 fit, each ≈317px); at 550px you get 2; at 300px, 1 — columns appear and disappear as the viewport changes, with zero breakpoints. The `min(260px, 100%)` guard handles the degenerate narrow case: on a 200px-wide container, a bare 260px minimum would force horizontal overflow, but `min(260px, 100%)` caps the minimum at the container width so the single column shrinks instead. Prefer `auto-fit` over `auto-fill` when empty tracks should collapse (cards stretch to fill), and `auto-fill` when you want placeholder tracks reserved so a lone item doesn't blow up to full width.

**مشکل:** عنصر فقط افقی وسط است و عمودی وسط نمی‌شود.

**علت:** `justify-content` روی محور اصلی (Main Axis) کار می‌کند و `align-items` روی محور عرضی (Cross Axis). برای وسط‌چین کامل هر دو لازم است. مهم‌تر اینکه والد باید ارتفاع داشته باشد؛ اگر ارتفاع والد auto باشد، خود والد به اندازه محتوا رشد کرده و عمودی وسط شدن معنا ندارد.

