```html
<div class="thumb-card">
  <img src="photo.jpg" alt="Mountain lake at sunrise" width="1200" height="800">
  <h3>Trip report</h3>
</div>

<style>
  .thumb-card img {
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: center;
    border-radius: 8px;
  }
</style>
```

**Why this works:** `aspect-ratio: 16 / 9` tells the browser the preferred width-to-height ratio of the box, so with `width: 100%` the height is *derived* — every card in a grid gets an identical image slot with zero JavaScript measuring and, critically, zero layout shift before the image loads (the box exists at the right size immediately). `object-fit: cover` then controls how the actual bitmap fills that box: `cover` scales the image up until it covers the whole box and crops the overflow, preserving the photo's own proportions (no stretching), while the default `fill` would distort it and `contain` would letterbox it. `object-position: center` picks which part survives the crop (e.g. `top` for portraits with heads). The HTML `width`/`height` attributes provide the browser's pre-CSS fallback ratio so even before the stylesheet lands, space is reserved — together the two mechanisms eliminate cumulative layout shift on media-heavy pages.

```js
const canvas = document.querySelector('canvas');
const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');

const ro = new ResizeObserver(entries => {
  for (const entry of entries) {
    // contentBoxSize ignores borders/padding; borderBoxSize includes them
    const { inlineSize: width, blockSize: height } = entry.contentBoxSize[0];
    canvas.width = width;   // canvas pixels = CSS size -> crisp rendering
    canvas.height = height;
    drawChart(width, height);
  }
});

ro.observe(canvas.parentElement); // react to the CONTAINER, not the window

function drawChart(w, h) {
  ctx.clearRect(0, 0, w, h);
  ctx.beginPath();
  ctx.moveTo(0, h / 2);
  ctx.lineTo(w, h / 2);
  ctx.stroke();
}

// Cleanup when the component goes away
// ro.disconnect();
```
`ResizeObserver` fires whenever an observed element's content box changes size — including container-driven changes that never touch the window (sidebar collapse, flex reflow, font loading). That is the foundation of container-responsive components, which media queries cannot express. Notes: the callback runs after layout but before paint (no flicker); it fires once on `observe()` with the initial size; entries arrive as an array (batched changes, one callback). Guard against resize loops: never change the observed element's own size inside its callback without a size check.

