```js
function createCounter(start = 0) {
  let count = start; // private variable captured by the closure
  return {
    increment() { return ++count; },
    decrement() { return --count; },
    value() { return count; }
  };
}

const counter = createCounter(10);
console.log(counter.increment()); // 11
console.log(counter.increment()); // 12
console.log(counter.value());     // 12
counter.count = 99;               // does NOT touch the real count
console.log(counter.value());     // still 12

// Closures also power factory functions:
const makeGreeter = (greeting) => (name) => `${greeting}, ${name}!`;
const sayHello = makeGreeter('Hello');
console.log(sayHello('Arion')); // Hello, Arion!
```
A closure is a function bundled with references to its surrounding lexical scope. Even after the outer function returns, inner functions keep access to its variables. Use closures for private state, factories and callbacks. Two caveats: closures capture variables (not values), so a variable that changes later is seen by every closure over it; and closures keep their captured variables in memory as long as the inner function is referenced.

```css
/* 1. Legibility scrim over a hero image */
.hero {
  background-image:
    linear-gradient(to top, rgb(0 0 0 / 0.7) 0%, transparent 55%),
    url('hero.jpg');
  background-size: cover;
}

/* 2. Fade-out text edge when content overflows */
.fade-clamp {
  mask-image: linear-gradient(to right, black 85%, transparent);
}

/* 3. Conic-gradient progress ring */
.ring {
  width: 96px;
  aspect-ratio: 1;
  border-radius: 50%;
  background:
    conic-gradient(var(--accent) 0 270deg, #e2e8f0 270deg 360deg);
  mask: radial-gradient(farthest-side, transparent 62%, black 63%);
}
```

**Why this works:** Gradients are generated images, so they stack in `background-image` layers like image URLs — the first listed layer paints on top, which is why the dark scrim sits above the photo and the `to top` direction keeps the dark side at the bottom. Using `rgb(0 0 0 / 0.7)` instead of a solid color makes the overlay blend with whatever is beneath. The fade-out edge uses a gradient as a `mask-image` (alpha mask): black = fully visible, transparent = hidden, so text dissolves exactly like native scroll-end fades without a PNG. The progress ring is two tricks combined: `conic-gradient` sweeps accent color over 270 of 360 degrees (75% progress), and the radial-gradient `mask` punches a hole in the middle, turning the disc into a ring — one element, no SVG, and the percentage is just an angle you can set from a custom property via `style="--p: 270deg"` if you extend the pattern.

**async/await — از مبتدی تا حرفه‌ای**

[مبتدی]
تابع `async` همیشه Promise برمی‌گرداند و `await` منتظر نتیجه می‌ماند.
```js
async function getUser() {
  const res = await fetch('/api/user');
  return res.json();
}
```
چرا کار می‌کند: `await` مقدار Promise را باز می‌کند تا کد خطی و خوانا بماند.

[متوسط]
خطاها را با `try/catch` بگیر و `res.ok` را چک کن چون fetch روی 404 خطا نمی‌دهد.
```js
try {
  const res = await fetch('/api/user');
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error('HTTP ' + res.status);
  const user = await res.json();
} catch (err) {
  console.error('خطا:', err.message);
}
```
چرا کار می‌کند: هم خطای شبکه و هم خطای HTTP در یک catch جمع می‌شوند.

[پیشرفته]
درخواست‌های مستقل را با `Promise.all` همزمان اجرا کن تا منتظر یکی‌یکی شدن نمانی.
```js
const [user, posts] = await Promise.all([
  fetch('/api/user').then(r => r.json()),
  fetch('/api/posts').then(r => r.json())
]);
```
چرا کار می‌کند: هر دو درخواست بلافاصله ارسال می‌شوند و زمان کل برابر کندترین آن‌هاست.

[حرفه‌ای]
با `AbortController` درخواست‌های قدیمی را لغو کن؛ مخصوصاً در search-as-you-type.
```js
let ctrl;
input.addEventListener('input', async () => {
  ctrl?.abort();
  ctrl = new AbortController();
  const res = await fetch('/api/search?q=' + encodeURIComponent(input.value), { signal: ctrl.signal });
});
```
چرا کار می‌کند: هر ورودی جدید درخواست قبلی را cancel می‌کند تا نتیجه همیشه تازه باشد.

