```js
const user = { name: 'Sara', address: { city: 'Tehran' }, tags: ['pro'] };

// ?. — short-circuits to undefined instead of throwing
console.log(user.address?.city);   // 'Tehran'
console.log(user.contact?.phone);  // undefined (old way: TypeError)
console.log(user.tags?.[0]);       // 'pro'
console.log(user.save?.());        // undefined if the method is missing

// ?? — falls back only on null/undefined, NOT on 0 or ''
const volume = 0;
console.log(volume ?? 50);         // 0  (correct: 0 is a real value)
console.log(volume || 50);         // 50 (|| treats 0 as falsy — bug!)

// Combined: config with defaults
const speed = user.settings?.scrollSpeed ?? 300;

// Optional call and short-circuit assignment
user.log?.('hi');
let cached;
cached ??= expensiveCompute();     // assigns only if null/undefined

// Pitfall: ?? mixed with || or && needs parentheses
// (user.name ?? 'anon').length   // OK
// user.name ?? 'anon'.length    // WRONG — parses as user.name ?? ('anon'.length)
```
`?.` guards property access, index access and function calls against `null`/`undefined`; everything after it is skipped if the left side is nullish. `??` supplies defaults only for `null`/`undefined` — unlike `||`, it keeps `0`, `''` and `false`. Mixing `??` with `||`/`&&` requires parentheses.

عملگر Spread عناصر یک آرایه یا خواص یک آبجکت را پخش می‌کند:

```js
// کپی آرایه (نه مرجع!)
const arr1 = [1, 2, 3];
const copy = [...arr1];
copy.push(4);
console.log(arr1); // [1, 2, 3] دست‌نخورده

// ادغام آرایه‌ها
const a = [1, 2], b = [3, 4];
const merged = [...a, ...b];        // [1, 2, 3, 4]
const withNew = [...a, 99, ...b];   // درج در وسط

// ادغام آبجکت‌ها (کلیدهای بعدی بر قبلی اولویت دارند)
const defaults = { theme: 'light', lang: 'fa' };
const userSettings = { lang: 'en' };
const settings = { ...defaults, ...userSettings };
// { theme: 'light', lang: 'en' }

// ارسال آرایه به‌عنوان آرگومان‌های تابع
const nums = [5, 2, 9];
console.log(Math.max(...nums)); // 9
```

برعکس آن Rest Parameter است که ورودی‌های تابع را جمع می‌کند:

```js
function sum(...numbers) {
  return numbers.reduce((s, n) => s + n, 0);
}
sum(1, 2, 3); // 6
```

هشدار مهم: Spread فقط کپی سطح اول (Shallow Copy) می‌سازد. اگر آرایه یا آبجکت تو در تو باشد، لایه‌های داخلی همچنان مرجع مشترک دارند:

```js
const nested = { info: { age: 25 } };
const clone = { ...nested };
clone.info.age = 30;
console.log(nested.info.age); // 30! کپی عمیق نشده
```

برای کپی عمیق واقعی از `structuredClone(obj)` استفاده کن.

```css
/* 1. Global kill-switch: neutralize decorative motion */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  *,
  *::before,
  *::after {
    animation-duration: 0.01ms !important;
    animation-iteration-count: 1 !important;
    transition-duration: 0.01ms !important;
    scroll-behavior: auto !important;
  }
}

/* 2. Better: enhance deliberately */
.fade-in {
  opacity: 1;
}

@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
  .fade-in {
    opacity: 0;
    animation: rise 0.6s ease-out forwards;
  }
  @keyframes rise {
    from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(12px); }
    to   { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
  }
}

/* 3. Pair with JS + CSS var for parallax */
:root { --parallax: 1; }
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  :root { --parallax: 0; }
}
/* JS: layer.style.transform = `translateY(${y * getComputedStyle(document.documentElement).getPropertyValue('--parallax')}px)` */
```

**Why this works:** The reduce media query matches users who asked their OS for less motion (vestibular disorders, motion sickness) — for them, flashing slideshows and parallax are not an aesthetic issue but a health issue. The blanket `0.01ms` pattern is the safe default because it ends animations *immediately* while still firing `animationend` events (fully zeroing duration can break JS that waits for those events) and stops infinite loops with `iteration-count: 1`. The progressive-enhancement variant (option 2) is architecturally cleaner: motion exists only inside `no-preference`, so reduced-motion users get the finished state by default with no `!important` arms race. For JS-driven effects, mirror the preference with `matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)')` or a CSS custom property the script reads, so CSS and JS never disagree. Note this replaces *decorative* motion only — functional feedback (e.g. a progress bar) should still exist, just without flourish.

Instead of attaching a listener to every child, attach one to the parent and inspect event.target.closest(). Fewer listeners, less memory, and it keeps working for elements added later.

```js
list.addEventListener("click", (e) => {
  const item = e.target.closest("li");
  if (item) item.classList.toggle("done");
});
```

```css
.grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(12, 1fr);
  gap: 24px;
  max-width: 1200px;
  margin-inline: auto;
}

.col-6  { grid-column: span 6; }
.col-4  { grid-column: span 4; }
.col-8  { grid-column: span 8; }
.col-12 { grid-column: span 12; }

@media (min-width: 768px) {
  .sm-col-4 { grid-column: span 4; }
}

/* Usage: two half-width cards + one full-width */
/* <div class="grid">
     <article class="col-12 col-6"></article>
     <article class="col-12 col-6"></article>
     <article class="col-12"></article>
   </div> */
```

**Why this works:** `repeat(12, 1fr)` creates twelve equal flexible tracks; `1fr` means each track gets an equal share of the space *left after* the gutters are removed, because `gap` reserves real track space — no more negative-margin + inner-padding gutter math from the float/Bootstrap 3 era. `grid-column: span N` makes an item stretch across N tracks; because the default flow is row-auto-placement, items automatically wrap to the next row when the remaining span doesn't fit. The mobile-first pattern works by listing the small-screen span first and overriding inside the media query — same specificity, later in source, so the cascade does the work without `!important`. Items remain stretch-aligned to their tracks by default, which is why cards in a row stay equal-height for free.

