Why it works: sticky is a hybrid — the element stays in normal flow until scrolling would push it past the top: 0 threshold, then it behaves like fixed within its nearest scrolling ancestor. The critical constraint people miss: a sticky element never leaves its parent, so each header sticks only while its own section is in view — which is exactly the effect you want for grouped lists, and it also means each section needs enough content to scroll for the stick to be visible. The most common bug is an ancestor with overflow: hidden/auto/scroll, which turns it into the scrolling container and eats the stickiness; another is forgetting that sticky needs a top (or bottom) value to do anything. The opaque background plus z-index keeps headings readable over the content scrolling beneath them.

