**Why this works:**
- `place-content: center` on the hero grid vertically and horizontally centers the content without absolute positioning.
- `clamp()` makes the headline fluid: it grows with the viewport between the min and max bounds, so no extra media query is needed.
- The navbar uses `backdrop-filter: blur()` over a semi-transparent background (via `color-mix`) for a glass effect while staying readable.
- The mobile menu is progressive enhancement: it is hidden by CSS below 720px and toggled with `aria-expanded` kept in sync for screen readers.
- Cards use `auto-fit` + `minmax(240px, 1fr)` so the grid reflows from 1 to 3 columns automatically.

@container lets a component style itself based on its *container's* size instead of the viewport, making truly reusable components. The parent needs container-type: inline-size to establish the query context.

```css
.card-host { container-type: inline-size; }
@container (min-width: 420px) {
  .card { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 96px 1fr; }
}
```

async functions always return a promise; await pauses the function (not the page) until it settles. Always pair awaits with try/catch or .catch — a rejected promise without handling becomes an unhandled rejection.

```js
async function load() {
  try {
    const res = await fetch("/api/items");
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(res.status);
    return await res.json();
  } catch (err) {
    console.error("load failed:", err);
    return [];
  }
}
```

```js
// Timers: schedule logic after delays — not visually smooth
setTimeout(() => console.log('once'), 500);
const id = setInterval(() => console.log('tick'), 1000);
clearInterval(id); // always clear timers you no longer need

// setInterval drifts and callbacks can pile up when the tab is busy.
// Safer self-scheduling pattern:
(function tick() {
  console.log('steady beat');
  setTimeout(tick, 1000); // next timer starts only after this run finishes
})();

// requestAnimationFrame: synced to display refresh (~60fps), paused in background tabs
const box = document.querySelector('.box');
function animate(durationMs) {
  const start = performance.now();
  function frame(now) {
    const t = Math.min((now - start) / durationMs, 1); // progress 0..1
    const eased = 1 - Math.pow(1 - t, 3);              // ease-out cubic
    box.style.transform = `translateX(${eased * 200}px)`;
    if (t < 1) requestAnimationFrame(frame);           // schedule next frame
  }
  requestAnimationFrame(frame);
}
animate(600);
```
Use timers for logic (debouncing, polling) and `requestAnimationFrame` for anything visual: it fires once per repaint, avoids tearing, and automatically throttles when the tab is hidden. Never animate with `setInterval` — intervals are not aligned with frames and can stack up.

