**DOM events — Beginner to Expert**

[Beginner]
Listen with `addEventListener` and react inside the callback.
```html
<button id="save">Save</button>
```
```js
document.querySelector('#save').addEventListener('click', () => {
  console.log('saved!');
});
```
Why it works: the browser calls your function every time the event fires on that element.

[Intermediate]
Use event delegation: one listener on a parent handles clicks for all current and future children.
```js
document.querySelector('#list').addEventListener('click', (e) => {
  const btn = e.target.closest('button.item');
  if (btn) console.log('clicked item', btn.dataset.id);
});
```
Why it works: clicks bubble up, and `closest()` finds the real target even on inner nodes.

[Advanced]
Handle inputs correctly: use `submit` with `preventDefault()` for forms, debounce expensive handlers, and clean up with `removeEventListener`.
```js
const form = document.querySelector('#search');
form.addEventListener('submit', (e) => {
  e.preventDefault();
  doSearch(new FormData(form));
});
```
Why it works: preventing default stops the page reload, and `FormData` collects all named fields.

[Expert]
Know the event lifecycle — capture, target, bubble — and use `{ once: true }`, `CustomEvent` with `detail`, and passive listeners for scroll performance.
```js
window.addEventListener('scroll', onScroll, { passive: true });
el.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('cart:add', { detail: { id: 7 } }));
```
Why it works: passive listeners promise not to call `preventDefault()`, letting the browser scroll without waiting for JS.

**Symptom:** the page scrolls left-right on phones (often only below ~500px) or shows a stray white strip on the right.

**Diagnosis — find the offender first:**
```js
// Paste in the console: outlines every element wider than the viewport
document.querySelectorAll('*').forEach(el => {
  const w = document.documentElement.clientWidth;
  if (el.scrollWidth > w || el.getBoundingClientRect().right > w + 1) {
    console.log(el, el.scrollWidth, el.getBoundingClientRect().right);
    el.style.outline = '2px solid red';
  }
});
```
**Common root causes:**
- Fixed pixel widths: `width: 400px` on a card inside a 360px viewport.
- `width: 100vw` — vw INCLUDES the scrollbar width, so it is wider than the visible area.
- Long unbreakable strings (URLs, emails) with no wrapping.
- Negative margins or transforms poking content outside the body.
- Images or `pre` blocks with intrinsic width.

**Fix:**
```css
html, body { overflow-x: clip; }          /* safety net, not the cure */
img, video, pre { max-width: 100%; }
p, li, a { overflow-wrap: break-word; }
.card { width: 100%; max-width: 400px; }  /* fluid, capped — never bare px width */
/* Also let flex/grid children shrink: min-width: 0 on the offending item */
```
Replace `100vw` with `100%` (or use `100dvw` deliberately).

**Prevention:** design with `max-width` instead of `width`; test at 320px early; keep the offender-detection snippet handy and run it on every layout change.

