```css
/* Reduce fallback layout shift with metric-compatible sizing */
@font-face {
  font-family: 'Inter-fallback';
  src: local('Arial');
  size-adjust: 107%;
  ascent-override: 90%;
}
body { font-family: 'Inter', 'Inter-fallback', sans-serif; }
```

Why it works: preload starts fetching the font CSS at the highest priority, before the parser would normally discover it. Because FOUT itself cannot be fully eliminated with display=swap, the second half of the fix is a fallback @font-face with size-adjust and ascent-override tuned so the fallback occupies nearly the same space as the real font — when the swap happens, text barely moves. This combination (early fetch + metric-matched fallback) is what tools like fontaine automate.

**Symptom:** on mobile, `height: 100vh` elements change size as the URL bar hides/shows while scrolling; full-screen sections are taller than what is actually visible.

**Root cause:** on mobile browsers `100vh` refers to the LARGEST viewport (toolbar hidden). While the toolbar is visible, the element is taller than the visible area; as you scroll, the browser hides/shows the toolbar, resizing the layout and causing the jump.

**Fix:**
```css
.hero {
  height: 100vh;   /* fallback for old browsers */
  height: 100svh;  /* small viewport: never taller than the visible area */
}
@supports (height: 100dvh) {
  .hero { height: 100dvh; }  /* tracks the CURRENT visible viewport */
}

/* JS fallback for legacy browsers: */
.hero { height: calc(var(--vh, 1vh) * 100); }
```
```js
const setVH = () =>
  document.documentElement.style.setProperty('--vh', `${window.innerHeight * 0.01}px`);
setVH();
addEventListener('resize', setVH, { passive: true }); // throttle in production
```
Unit guide: `svh` = smallest viewport (stable, may leave a gap when the toolbar hides), `lvh` = largest (classic 100vh behavior), `dvh` = dynamic (updates live — it changes WHILE scrolling, so avoid attaching expensive effects to it).

**Prevention:** default full-screen sections to `min-height: 100svh`; reserve `dvh` for elements that must truly track the visible viewport; test in Chrome DevTools device mode with a mobile toolbar profile.

**Advanced fetch — Beginner to Expert**

[Beginner]
Wrap fetch in a function so every call gets consistent error handling.
```js
async function getJSON(url) {
  const res = await fetch(url);
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error('HTTP ' + res.status);
  return res.json();
}
```
Why it works: centralizing the `res.ok` check turns silent 404s into catchable errors.

[Intermediate]
Add a timeout with `AbortSignal.timeout` or `AbortController`.
```js
async function getJSON(url, ms = 5000) {
  const res = await fetch(url, { signal: AbortSignal.timeout(ms) });
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error('HTTP ' + res.status);
  return res.json();
}
```
Why it works: the signal aborts the request after `ms`, so hangs become timeout errors.

[Advanced]
Add exponential backoff retry for transient failures only (network, 502/503/429).
```js
async function retry(fn, tries = 3) {
  for (let i = 0; i < tries; i++) {
    try { return await fn(); }
    catch (e) {
      if (i === tries - 1) throw e;
      await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 2 ** i * 300));
    }
  }
}
const data = await retry(() => getJSON('/api/list'));
```
Why it works: waiting 300ms, 600ms, 1200ms gives a struggling server room to recover.

[Expert]
Cancel stale requests when inputs change (search-as-you-type).
```js
let ctrl;
input.addEventListener('input', async () => {
  ctrl?.abort();
  ctrl = new AbortController();
  try {
    const res = await fetch('/api/search?q=' + encodeURIComponent(input.value), { signal: ctrl.signal });
    render(await res.json());
  } catch (e) { if (e.name !== 'AbortError') console.error(e); }
});
```
Why it works: each keystroke aborts the previous query, so results always match the newest input.

```js
// Map: keys of ANY type, insertion order, direct size
const visits = new Map();
visits.set('sara', 3);
visits.set(42, 'answer');
visits.set({ id: 1 }, 'object keys work');
console.log(visits.get('sara'), visits.size); // 3 3
console.log(visits.has(42));                  // true
visits.delete(42);
for (const [key, value] of visits) console.log(key, value);

// Convert between objects and Maps
const obj = { a: 1, b: 2 };
const m = new Map(Object.entries(obj));       // obj -> Map
const back = Object.fromEntries(m);           // Map -> obj

// Set: unique values, O(1) add/has/delete
const ids = new Set([1, 2, 2, 3]);
console.log(ids.size);                        // 3
ids.add(3).add(4);
console.log([...ids]);                        // [1, 2, 3, 4]

const uniq = arr => [...new Set(arr)];
console.log(uniq(['a', 'b', 'a']));           // ['a', 'b']

const setA = new Set([1, 2, 3]);
const common = [1, 2, 3].filter(x => new Set([2, 3, 4]).has(x)); // intersection [2, 3]
```
Use `Map` when keys are not strings, when you add/delete keys frequently, need `size`, or iterate in insertion order. Use `Set` for deduplication and membership tests — much cleaner than `arr.indexOf(x) !== -1`. Object keys are always strings/symbols; Map keys keep their type (even objects and NaN).

