```html
<form id="contact-form" novalidate>
  <input name="email" type="email" required placeholder="ایمیل">
  <textarea name="message" required minlength="10" placeholder="پیام"></textarea>
  <button type="submit">ارسال</button>
  <p class="status" role="status"></p>
</form>
```

```js
const form = document.querySelector('#contact-form');
const status = form.querySelector('.status');

form.addEventListener('submit', async (e) => {
  e.preventDefault();

  if (!form.checkValidity()) {
    form.reportValidity();
    return;
  }

  status.textContent = 'در حال ارسال…';

  try {
    const res = await fetch('/api/contact', {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
      body: JSON.stringify(Object.fromEntries(new FormData(form))),
    });

    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);

    status.textContent = 'پیام شما ارسال شد ✔';
    form.reset();
  } catch {
    status.textContent = 'ارسال ناموفق بود؛ دوباره تلاش کن.';
  }
});
```

نکته‌ها:
- `novalidate` روی فرم + چک دستی `checkValidity()` یعنی کنترل کاملاً دست خودته ولی از پیام‌های بومی مرورگر (reportValidity) هم استفاده می‌کنی
- `role="status"` باعث می‌شه اسکرین‌ریدر پیام موفقیت/خطا رو خودکار اعلام کنه
- دکمه رو موقع ارسال disable کن تا کاربر دوبار کلیک نکنه

```css
.banner {
  background: linear-gradient(270deg, #8b5cf6, #ec4899, #f59e0b, #8b5cf6);
  background-size: 600% 600%;
  animation: shift 12s ease infinite;
}
@keyframes shift {
  0%   { background-position: 0% 50%; }
  50%  { background-position: 100% 50%; }
  100% { background-position: 0% 50%; }
}
```

Why it works: a CSS gradient is treated as an image, so it can be sized and positioned. The gradient is painted at 600% of the element width — far larger than what is visible — and the animation slides background-position across it. Because the first and last color stops are the same (#8b5cf6 ... #8b5cf6) and the keyframes return to the starting position, the loop is seamless with no visible jump. Animating background-position instead of the gradient itself means no repaint of new images per frame; the browser just moves an existing painted layer, which stays cheap.

```js
const original = { a: 1, nested: { b: 2 }, list: [3, 4], when: new Date(0) };

// 1. Spread / Object.assign: SHALLOW — nested objects are shared
const shallow = { ...original };
shallow.nested.b = 99;
console.log(original.nested.b); // 99 — changed too! Same reference

// 2. structuredClone: TRUE deep copy, handles Dates, Maps, Sets, cycles
const deep = structuredClone(original);
deep.nested.b = 42;
console.log(original.nested.b); // 99 — unaffected by the deep edit

// 3. JSON round-trip: deep but LOSSY — do not use for real data
const lossy = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(original));
console.log(lossy.when); // '1970-01-01T00:00:00.000Z' — Date became a string
// undefined values, functions, Infinity are dropped; Map/Set are mangled

// 4. Manual clone for known shapes (fast, explicit)
function cloneUser(u) {
  return { ...u, nested: { ...u.nested }, list: [...u.list] };
}
```
Guidelines: spread/`Object.assign` copy one level — fine for flat state updates (React/Redux style). Use `structuredClone` (native since 2022) for deep copies; it throws on functions and DOM nodes. Avoid `JSON.parse(JSON.stringify())` in production: it silently destroys Dates, `undefined`, functions and Maps, and throws on circular references. If you are cloning on every change at scale, consider immutable update patterns instead.

**async/await — Beginner to Expert**

[Beginner]
An `async` function always returns a promise, and `await` pauses inside it until a promise settles.
```js
async function getUser() {
  const res = await fetch('/api/user');
  return res.json();
}
getUser().then(console.log);
```
Why it works: `await` unwraps the promise value, so the code reads top-to-bottom like sync code.

[Intermediate]
Always handle failures with `try/catch`, and check `res.ok` since fetch does not throw on 404/500.
```js
async function getUser() {
  try {
    const res = await fetch('/api/user');
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error('HTTP ' + res.status);
    return await res.json();
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Failed:', err.message);
    return null;
  }
}
```
Why it works: the catch block receives network errors and thrown HTTP errors in one place.

[Advanced]
Run independent awaits concurrently with `Promise.all`, or `allSettled` when partial failure is acceptable.
```js
const [user, posts] = await Promise.all([
  fetch('/api/user').then(r => r.json()),
  fetch('/api/posts').then(r => r.json())
]);
```
Why it works: both requests start immediately and the await finishes when the slowest settles.

[Expert]
Control lifetime with `AbortController` and timeouts, and serialize only when there is a real dependency.
```js
const ctrl = new AbortController();
const timer = setTimeout(() => ctrl.abort(), 5000);
try {
  const res = await fetch('/api/search?q=css', { signal: ctrl.signal });
  clearTimeout(timer);
  return await res.json();
} catch (e) {
  return { error: e.name === 'AbortError' ? 'timeout' : 'network' };
}
```
Why it works: aborting cancels the in-flight request, freeing the connection instead of waiting forever.

```javascript
async function getUserData(userId) {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(`https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/users/${userId}`);

    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error(`خطا در دریافت داده: ${response.status}`);
    }

    const userData = await response.json();
    console.log('درباره کاربر:', userData.name);
    console.log('ایمیل کاربر:', userData.email);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('خطا:', error.message);
  }
}

getUserData(1);
```

در این مثال، تابع `getUserData` با استفاده از `async/await` داده‌های یک کاربر را از API دریافت می‌کند. کلیدواژه `async` تابع را به صورت async تعریف می‌کند و `await` منتظر پاسخ API می‌ماند. 

چرا از `try/catch` استفاده کردیم؟ چون عملیات شبکه ممکن است با خطا مواجه شود (مثل قطعی اینترنت یا خطای سرور). `response.ok` بررسی می‌کند که درخواست موفقیت‌آمیز بوده است. 

نکته مهم: `await` فقط روی Promiseها کار می‌کند و `fetch` یک Promise برمی‌گرداند. این روش خوانایی کد را به شدت افزایش می‌دهد و از callback hell جلوگیری می‌کند.

```js
class ValidationError extends Error {
  constructor(field, message) {
    super(message);
    this.name = 'ValidationError';  // appears in stack traces
    this.field = field;
    this.status = 400;
  }
}

class ApiError extends Error {
  constructor(status, body) {
    super(`API responded with ${status}`);
    this.name = 'ApiError';
    this.status = status;
    this.body = body;
  }
}

function validateEmail(email) {
  if (!/^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/.test(email)) {
    throw new ValidationError('email', 'Invalid email format');
  }
}

async function saveUser(data) {
  try {
    validateEmail(data.email);  // sync throw
    const res = await fetch('/api/users', { method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify(data) });
    if (!res.ok) throw new ApiError(res.status, await res.text());
    return await res.json();
  } catch (err) {
    if (err instanceof ValidationError) {
      showFieldError(err.field, err.message); // recoverable: flag the input
      return null;
    }
    throw err; // unknown: rethrow, log at the top level
  } finally {
    console.log('runs on success, catch, AND rethrow');
  }
}
```
Extend `Error` and call `super(message)` so you get proper stacks and `instanceof` still works. Use distinct error types to separate handling decisions (retry a 503, flag a 400 field, crash on genuine bugs). `finally` runs no matter how the block exits — ideal for closing loaders or connections. Never swallow errors silently; catch only what you can meaningfully handle and rethrow the rest.

