```html
<!-- Option B: link in the document head — preferred -->
<link rel='preconnect' href='https://fonts.googleapis.com'>
<link rel='preconnect' href='https://fonts.gstatic.com' crossorigin>
<link href='https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Inter:wght@400;700&display=swap' rel='stylesheet'>
```

Why it works: @import is only discovered after the browser downloads and parses your CSS file, which serializes the requests — CSS file, then font CSS, then font files (a waterfall). The link tag is found immediately when the HTML head is parsed, so the font CSS starts downloading in parallel with your own stylesheets. On slow connections the @import pattern can delay first paint by hundreds of milliseconds. Use @import only in quick prototypes; in production always use link (or bundle/self-host the fonts).

```js
async function getUser(id) {
  const res = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`); // await pauses THIS function, not the page
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
  return res.json();
}

// Sequential (slower: the second request waits for the first)
async function sequential() {
  const a = await getUser(1);
  const b = await getUser(2);
  return [a, b];
}

// Parallel (faster: both requests are in flight together)
async function parallel() {
  const [a, b] = await Promise.all([getUser(1), getUser(2)]);
  return [a, b];
}

// Error handling: sync throws and rejected awaits are caught the same way
async function run() {
  try {
    const user = await getUser(999);
    console.log(user);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Failed:', err.message); // HTTP 404, network error, bad JSON...
  } finally {
    console.log('cleanup here');
  }
}
run();
```
Key points: `async` functions always return a promise; `await` works on any thenable; `try/catch` handles both sync and async errors inside the function. Never `await` in a loop for independent requests — use `Promise.all`. And always consume the promise an async function returns; a floating call with no `await`/`.catch` can produce an unhandled rejection.

